<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Germiyanids</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1429 CE (approx.) · capital Kütahya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful beylik of western Anatolia centred on Kutahya, important patrons of early Anatolian Turkish literature.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Germiyan were a tribal group settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/melid">Malatya</a> and moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutahya">Kutahya</a> region in the later 13th century to guard the frontier. As Seljuk authority waned around 1300, their leaders ruled Kutahya as an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>, for a time the strongest in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Yakub I received tribute from several neighbours, including Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alasehir">Philadelphia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> later absorbed the Sahib Ataid and Inancid lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleyman-shah-of-germiyan">Suleyman Shah</a> married his daughter to the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a>, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutahya">Kutahya</a> as part of her dowry around 1381. The court sponsored poets writing in Turkish, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedi">Ahmedi</a> and Sheykhoglu Mustafa, and the alum and other mines of the region brought wealth.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> took the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> in 1390, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yakub-ii-of-germiyan">Yakub II</a> in 1402. Yakub II bequeathed his lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>, and they passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> on his death in 1429.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/germiyanids.jpg" alt="Germiyanids" width="320" height="198"><figcaption>Image: Gabagool · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anatolia1300.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1429 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kütahya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yakub I, Suleyman Shah, Yakub II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Germiyanid Beylik, Germiyanoğulları</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1364&amp;focus=germiyanids" aria-label="Germiyanids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5421 765 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Germiyanids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5741 1021L5810 1072M5741 1021L5709 970M5741 1021L6582 1190M5741 1021L5698 1059M5741 1021L5758 1043M5741 1021L5726 1075"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5810 1072h0M5709 970h0M6582 1190h0M5698 1059h0M5758 1043h0M5726 1075h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5741 1021h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5741 1021h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kütahya. <a href="/atlas?year=1364&amp;focus=germiyanids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
